WEBVTT

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.700
A dependency is something a service needs
to do its job.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:08.475
Follow the arrows from the shop’s web
service to the API,

00:00:08.475 --> 00:00:12.675
then through the database service to the
server called pg-shop.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.425
An observation is one report from one
source.

00:00:17.425 --> 00:00:20.300
Kubernetes names the shop’s services.

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.975
Network records show calls between them.

00:00:22.975 --> 00:00:25.000
Azure lists the database server,

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.900
and its meter tells us whether the server
answered.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:33.175
A health model is a map of what’s healthy
and who depends on it.

00:00:33.175 --> 00:00:36.425
Sattle joins the observations in a master
graph.

00:00:36.425 --> 00:00:38.475
One query draws the shop.

00:00:38.475 --> 00:00:40.625
A second applies your health rules.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.825
Scope tells Sattle where to look.

00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:47.125
The replay comes with working rules.

00:00:47.125 --> 00:00:50.275
For your own shop, connect the sources you
can read,

00:00:50.275 --> 00:00:54.150
then choose the cluster and the namespace
that holds your services.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.700
A signal rule turns readings into an
alarm.

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:01.375
Your team sets the threshold and the wait.

00:01:01.375 --> 00:01:05.850
For servers like pg-shop, one bad minute
raises the alarm.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.375
Two good minutes clear it.

00:01:08.908 --> 00:01:12.558
Your team also chooses which failures
should affect a caller.

00:01:12.558 --> 00:01:15.083
The API needs its database.

00:01:15.083 --> 00:01:18.558
If your own API can serve a request
without its cache,

00:01:18.558 --> 00:01:20.933
you can leave that call out of its health
rule.

00:01:22.892 --> 00:01:26.692
Identity tells Sattle when two records
describe the same thing.

00:01:26.692 --> 00:01:28.717
Compare the Azure resource ID in the

00:01:28.717 --> 00:01:31.792
inventory with the ID on the meter
reading.

00:01:31.792 --> 00:01:32.942
They match.

00:01:32.942 --> 00:01:35.542
Both records belong to pg-shop.

00:01:37.050 --> 00:01:40.150
A projection is a query that builds a new
view.

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.175
Here, several copies of the web service
call the API.

00:01:44.175 --> 00:01:48.275
The projection groups those calls into one
arrow between the services.

00:01:49.825 --> 00:01:52.775
An ontology is the map’s vocabulary.

00:01:52.775 --> 00:01:56.175
It names kinds of things, like a service
or a server,

00:01:56.175 --> 00:01:58.050
and the links they can have.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.900
Here the database service uses a server
called pg-shop.

00:02:03.825 --> 00:02:07.725
A time series keeps readings in the order
of their sample times.

00:02:07.725 --> 00:02:11.125
At 10:11, pg-shop reports zero.

00:02:11.125 --> 00:02:14.475
The recorder gets that sample a minute and
a second later.

00:02:14.475 --> 00:02:16.575
The chart puts it at 10:11.

00:02:18.117 --> 00:02:20.692
Impact travels back toward the callers.

00:02:20.692 --> 00:02:24.117
Start at pg-shop and follow the dots to
the left.

00:02:24.117 --> 00:02:26.417
The alarm reaches the database service,

00:02:26.417 --> 00:02:29.692
the API, then the gateway and web service.

00:02:29.692 --> 00:02:31.417
The cache stays healthy.

00:02:32.975 --> 00:02:35.700
At 10:14 the meter rises to one.

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:37.375
That’s the first good minute.

00:02:37.375 --> 00:02:39.950
Pause there and the map is still red.

00:02:39.950 --> 00:02:44.800
At 10:15 the next good minute clears the
alarm, and the callers turn green.

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:50.992
Degraded means the model sees trouble that
falls short of an outage.

00:02:50.992 --> 00:02:54.467
Earlier in the replay, the cache comes
under heavy load.

00:02:54.467 --> 00:02:57.217
The web service uses it and turns amber.

00:02:57.217 --> 00:02:58.992
The gateway stays green.

00:03:00.558 --> 00:03:03.908
Evidence is a record that supports
something on the map.

00:03:03.908 --> 00:03:06.983
Give the API the existing cache address.

00:03:06.983 --> 00:03:09.983
Sattle matches that setting to Azure
inventory

00:03:09.983 --> 00:03:12.908
and adds an arrow from the API to the
cache.

00:03:14.425 --> 00:03:18.100
Compare the gateways at 10:06:30.

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:21.100
In the original run, the gateway is
healthy.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:25.600
In the changed run, it depends on the busy
cache through the API.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.775
The same readings now give the gateway an
amber light.

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:35.025
Suppose the API now names a different
cache, offers-cache.

00:03:35.025 --> 00:03:37.800
The setting gives Sattle an address to
look for.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:40.400
Until Azure reports a resource at that
address,

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.275
the shop map has no target for the new
arrow.

00:03:44.875 --> 00:03:48.000
Now Azure Resource Graph lists the new
cache.

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.775
Its address matches the API setting.

00:03:50.775 --> 00:03:53.450
Since the shop already uses this kind of
cache,

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:55.875
the existing query adds the new link.

00:03:57.483 --> 00:04:01.433
Unknown means the model has too little
evidence to judge health.

00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:03.908
Azure tells us offers-cache exists.

00:04:03.908 --> 00:04:05.658
We still need its readings.

00:04:05.658 --> 00:04:09.758
A new kind of resource may also need a new
matching rule or meter.

00:04:11.675 --> 00:04:13.775
A patch is a list of changes.

00:04:13.775 --> 00:04:15.650
When a source reports an update,

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:20.100
the engine sends the affected parts to the
Canvas, Sattle’s viewer.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.850
A gap in reporting leaves the last known
resource on the map.

00:04:25.408 --> 00:04:28.458
A revision marks a point in the history of
the map.

00:04:28.458 --> 00:04:31.483
The Canvas can return to it by taking a
saved map

00:04:31.483 --> 00:04:33.783
and applying the next few patches.

00:04:33.783 --> 00:04:37.333
In this replay, revision 16 shows the
outage.

00:04:37.333 --> 00:04:39.683
Revision 18 shows recovery.

00:04:41.242 --> 00:04:46.317
To test a replay, Sattle feeds the same
records and rules to two programs.

00:04:46.317 --> 00:04:48.992
One updates the graph as records arrive.

00:04:48.992 --> 00:04:51.167
The other rebuilds it from scratch.

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:54.167
The white paper explains when their
answers should agree.

00:04:56.117 --> 00:04:59.142
An origin has a failing signal of its own.

00:04:59.142 --> 00:05:04.267
In the Canvas workload tree, select
pg-shop at revision 16.

00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:07.417
Its origin marker singles out the
database.

00:05:07.417 --> 00:05:10.967
The web service is red because it depends
on that database.

00:05:12.517 --> 00:05:15.442
Select the database link in the Inspector.

00:05:15.442 --> 00:05:16.642
Follow it through the health

00:05:16.642 --> 00:05:20.342
and structure queries to the recorded
names and IDs.

00:05:20.342 --> 00:05:24.167
Those records let a teammate check why
Sattle connected the service

00:05:24.167 --> 00:05:25.892
to this particular server.

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.950
To try a different rule, open the
projection editor on a running engine.

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.650
Edit a draft, then preview its rows.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:37.800
You can see what your query would return
before you save it.
